</node>
<node id="Attention Mechanisms in Transformer Architecture (image)">
  <data key="d0">Attention Mechanisms in Transformer Architecture (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 2 illustrates the concepts of Scaled Dot-Product Attention and Multi-Head Attention, key components of the Transformer architecture. It visually represents the flow of data through these mechanisms, complementing the surrounding text that details the encoder and decoder stacks and their functionalities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ee7ddfc904c3325ec501459694c189ff</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763839326</data>
</node>
<node id="Scaled Dot-Product Attention (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Scaled Dot-Product Attention (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the scaled dot-product attention mechanism, which computes a weighted sum of values based on the compatibility of queries and keys, essential for the Transformer model's ability to capture contextual relationships in sequences.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a2ae2632555e53b43dea715eb694853b</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763839326</data>
</node>
<node id="Variance of Dot Product in Attention Mechanism (discarded)">
  <data key="d0">Variance of Dot Product in Attention Mechanism (discarded)</data>
  <data key="d1">discarded</data>
  <data key="d2">This content illustrates the statistical properties of the dot product of vectors in attention mechanisms, explaining why large dot products can occur. It relates to the scaling factor used in attention functions to mitigate issues with gradient saturation, supporting the broader discussion on attention mechanisms in the Transformer architecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e958a949941143a13ac2dd9bf72cd0c</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763839326</data>
</node>
