  <data key="d3">chunk-b69bf1f7cb958031a524a5d59e4b5d21</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763839326</data>
</node>
<node id="Comparative Analysis of Layer Types in Neural Networks (table)">
  <data key="d0">Comparative Analysis of Layer Types in Neural Networks (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">This table compares the computational complexity, sequential operations, and maximum path lengths of self-attention, recurrent, and convolutional layers. It illustrates the advantages of self-attention in efficiently managing long-range dependencies, supporting the surrounding content's emphasis on the effectiveness of multi-head attention in the Transformer architecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eff08634168e96b691200979bb1dcad2</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763839326</data>
</node>
<node id="Positional Encoding Functions (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Positional Encoding Functions (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the sinusoidal positional encodings used in the Transformer model to inject order information into token embeddings, enabling effective learning of sequence relationships in NLP tasks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-53642544e01ed81803d13bd9ce39deed</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763839326</data>
</node>
